# Hypothesis

**Summed social capital peaks in the 14th century BCE (1400–1301 BCE), and the increase is driven by
Copper&Gold together with Monument Count — not by Amber.**

Using the equal-weight sum of the three standardised social-capital series
(`CopperGold_inter + Amber_inter + MonumentCount_inter`), the maximum is reached in the century bin
1400–1301 BCE (14th c. BCE; sum = **4.238 Z**), ahead of 1300–1201 BCE (3.933), 1200–1101 BCE (3.622) and
1500–1401 BCE (1.924). Century convention: astronomical year numbering with no year 0 and conventional
BCE century boundaries (century *n* BCE = 100·n … 100·n−99 BCE, i.e. `century = floor(CE/100)*100`), the
same convention the source row labels use. Because the source data are themselves 100-year bins, the
uninterpolated grid returns an **identical maximum (4.238) in both the 14th and 13th centuries BCE**;
light smoothing (≤100 yr) keeps the label on the 14th c. BCE, heavy smoothing (200–500 yr) moves it to the
13th. The defensible statement is therefore a **plateau spanning 1400–1200 BCE, centred on the 14th c. BCE**
(Late Bronze Age), and any single-century label is convention-dependent rather than a property of the data.

**Components.** Copper&Gold and Monument Count are the components that genuinely increase into this peak,
and they flag as increasing under both contrast windows tested (full span, and pre-peak baseline
2500–2101 BCE → peak window 1400–1201 BCE). Copper&Gold contributes **62%** of the summed rise from the
baseline to the peak (Δ = +2.82 Z; value in the peak century +2.54, rank 3/37 in its own series, own
maximum +2.85 in the 12th c. BCE) and rises robustly across all three full-span endpoint definitions
(+0.19 to +0.33). Monument Count contributes **35%** (Δ = +1.59 Z; +1.71 in the peak century, rank 3/37)
and also rises robustly (+0.38 to +0.41); measured from the summed curve's own trough (20th c. BCE) the
rise splits almost evenly between the two (41% / 41%). Measured against the peak, these two series are
*still climbing* — neither has its own maximum in the peak century.

**Amber is the component that does NOT increase** in any meaningful sense, and it is also the one place
where the two windows disagree: it shows a nominal +0.32 Z from the pre-peak baseline to the peak window
(only **7%** of the summed rise), but in the peak century it sits at −0.01 Z, rank 14/37 — mid-pack in its
own series — its true maximum (+1.54) lies ~1900 years earlier in the 33rd c. BCE, and the sign of its
full-span change is **not robust** (+0.27 UP on raw grid endpoints vs −0.98 and −1.83 DOWN on interpolated
century means). Amber is best read as an early-Neolithic background signal, not a driver of the peak, and
we explicitly decline to state a full-span direction for it.

**Demographic covariate.** Pollen openness (Belau+Woserin PC1) was used only as a covariate, over the
shared window 4100–4001 to 800–701 BCE (34 century bins; pollen ends 702 BCE, ~200 yr before the
archaeological record, so the last two archaeological centuries are excluded from all pollen statistics).
Openness is essentially uncorrelated with summed social capital there (Pearson r = −0.075, Spearman
ρ = −0.220, first-difference r = −0.140; no lag in ±500 yr exceeds |r| = 0.23), so **no lead/lag or
driving relationship is identifiable**. Openness is mid-range in the social-capital peak century
(+1.40, rank 12/34), while its own maximum (+4.82) falls in the 8th c. BCE — the last century bin that
contains pollen data. That is a record-truncation edge, so it cannot date a demographic maximum; all we
claim is that the social-capital peak is *not* explained as a simple population maximum, since very high
openness coexists with low social capital late in the sequence.

**Interpretation.** The hypothesis is that summed social capital in this record crests in the 14th century
BCE (plateau 1400–1200 BCE) as a **metallurgical and monumental-effort phenomenon**: long-distance/precious-metal prestige goods (Copper&Gold) and the capacity to mobilise labour for monuments (Monument Count)
stay flat-to-negative through 2000–1600 BCE (Copper&Gold +0.03→−0.36, Monument Count −0.82→+0.17) and then jump together in the 15th–14th centuries BCE (Copper&Gold +1.08 then +2.54; Monument Count +0.93 then +1.71), cresting in the Late Bronze Age, while symbolically/ritually coded
material (its peak is also 14th c. BCE) co-varies and neither amber nor population pressure accounts for
it. Note the cross-group caveat: Sickle (SD 0.55) and MonumentSize (SD 0.67) are not standardised to SD≈1,
so group sums are compared only in *direction* and peak century (social and symbolic both peak in the
14th c. BCE; economic in the 11th c. BCE; cultural in the 31st c. BCE), never in magnitude. All statements
are descriptive (rankings, plateaus, sensitivity ranges); the files contain no dating uncertainty,
replication or measurement error, so no significance test is offered.
